A||||5||WisconsinGT||Need some advice
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||07-23-99||05:04 PM||||OK! I just got my GT back. Had a local place do it. (They will never do it again.) Four hours later, I start it up. It now has a very loud tick, tick, tick sound. They told me that I have a brocken bolt on my front exhaust manafold. But I never heard this loud of a ticking sound before. The reason I brought it in to be fixed, is because the EGR was seperated from the crossover pipe. So I bought a new crossover pipe. Is it possible that closing one leak, would make this kind of noise from another leak? <p>Thank you,<br>Brian
Z||000001||WisconsinGT||07-23-99||07:57 PM||||Sorry folks! It turns out that the dumb #*&$^ did not put the crossover pipe on correctly. Seems to be leaking. Anyway, if I have a broken exhaust manafold bolt, do I need to drop the engine to get it out?<p>
Z||000002||WisconsinGT||07-23-99||07:57 PM||||Sorry folks! It turns out that the dumb #*&$^ did not put the crossover pipe on correctly. Seems to be leaking. Anyway, if I have a broken exhaust manafold bolt, do I need to drop the engine to get it out?<p>
Z||000003||PaUL||07-23-99||08:08 PM||||from what you describe that ticking sound is definitely an exhaust leak. You dont have to drop the whole engine to change the exhaust manifold bolts. The worse case scenario would be if the broken bolt was on the bulkhead side head and it was jammed in there good. In that case you may have to remove that head and drill it out, then tap new theads. Theres hardly any room on that head to work on it. 
Z||000004||JetroGT||07-23-99||10:43 PM||||I just had the heads replaced on my SE about two months ago. It cost me about $980 in just labor. If you have the time/tools to do it your self, go for it. It took them about a week. And the job they did was a bit short from perfect.
Z||000005||Speadraser||07-29-99||06:05 PM||||I recently pulled my heads off my 86 SE for a complete rebuild and it only took me about 6 hours to remove them.  I am in germany and carried them as my carry-on luggage to mississippi where I was on business for two weeks.  The local machine shop charged me $225 for a complete valve job including new guides and teflon seals.  $20 of the cost was two broken exhast bolts.  Remember to use new gaskets on everything!  It might be a good idea to weld and port your exhast manifolds so they don't cause any problems in the future.  Good luck!
